00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:56.220
Today's material does not carry a price for those listings. Here is how it works when the question

00:04:56.220 --> 00:05:02.600
is whether a probability was right. You scored across many events, not one call. If a method

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:09.280
says 60%, calibration means that class of events arrives about 60% of the time. Sharpness

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:16.200
is different. It is how much probability sits near 0 or near 1 instead of near 50%. A sharp

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:22.700
forecast can still be poorly calibrated. A calibrated one can stay vague. One surprised does not

00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:27.320
make the forecast wrong because the score comes after the set of events has resolved.

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:31.120
The papers in hand are weather studies, not market studies.

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:37.140
Monthly Weather Review 2013 describes an improved version of Ensemble Kernel-Density

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:40.400
Model Output Statistics, EKD-MOS.

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:46.220
Its model output statistics equations are multiple linear regressions that relate observations

00:05:46.220 --> 00:05:48.780
to predictors built from the Ensemble mean.

00:05:48.780 --> 00:05:54.260
It's spread skill link is a one-term linear regression that predicts how accurate that

00:05:54.260 --> 00:05:56.260
mean should be given the spread.

00:05:56.560 --> 00:06:01.840
The method runs those equations on each member, fits a probability density with kernels,

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:06.040
then rescales the density spread to what the skill relationship predicts.

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:11.740
On two meter temperature forecasts from the North American Ensemble forecast system, October

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:15.840
1, 2007, through March 31, 2010.

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:21.520
The improved forecasts were more reliable than an older spread adjustment and then ranks

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:23.080
sorting bias corrected members.

00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:28.380
They also had a better match between spread and error and the spread varied more from

00:06:28.380 --> 00:06:28.980
day to day.

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:35.120
Weather and forecasting, 2010, compared bias correction with spatial disaggregation against

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:40.500
an analog method, using daily precipitation from the European Center for Medium-Range Weather

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:41.700
forecasts, ensemble.

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:49.820
The test covered a 10-day forecast window over the Ohio River Basin, 2002 through 2006, checked

00:06:49.820 --> 00:06:52.700
against tropical rainfall monitoring mission observations.

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:57.140
Neither method beat a simple spatial interpolation benchmark on skill.

00:06:57.820 --> 00:07:03.940
Both improved reliability, and the analog improved it more, bias correction improved bias at every

00:07:03.940 --> 00:07:09.180
forecast amount, though less at the large amounts, and the downscaled patterns looked unrealistic.

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:14.840
The analog cut bias across a wider range of amounts and drew more realistic patterns.

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:21.440
On a market, reliability is whether the implied probability matches later frequency, and sharpness

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:24.060
is how far the price sits from 50%.

00:07:24.460 --> 00:07:28.940
A repair can lift reliability and still lose to a plain benchmark on skill.

00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:34.100
One contract resolving the other way does not, by itself, settle calibration.

00:07:34.820 --> 00:07:37.840
That includes a thin market and it includes play money.

